TP.register({id:"lp8-g4",lp:8,grade:"4",mode:"curriculum",checks:7,nextGrade:"5th grade",build(E){const F=E.F;const FLU_TITLE="The Rain Gauge";const FLU=["Mr. Haddad keeps a rain gauge on a short post behind the school greenhouse. It stands in open ground, well away from the building and the trees. It is a plain plastic tube with lines printed up the side. It has stood there for eleven years.","Every school morning, one student from the fourth grade walks out to read it. You have to crouch down until your eye is level with the water. Looking down from above makes the number look bigger than it really is. Then you write the number on the chart by the greenhouse door. Then you tip the tube out.","Nobody believes the chart matters until the last week of April. That is when Mr. Haddad lays all eleven charts side by side on the long table. The class works out which spring was the wettest. Last year it was close, and it came down to one afternoon in March."].join("\n\n");const STORY_TITLE="The Second Tray";const STORY=["Elena's tray was the only one in the greenhouse with nothing in it.","She had planted her radish seeds on a Tuesday, the same day as everybody else, and then she had watered them every day, twice a day, because she wanted to be sure. By the end of the second week the other trays had small green rows across them. Hers had a dull green crust on top of the soil and nothing else.","Mr. Haddad looked at it for a while without saying anything.","“How often?” he asked.","“Twice a day.”","“Ah.” He pushed one finger into the soil up to the knuckle and held it out to her. It came up wet and dark. “A seed is not a fish,” he said. “Roots need air as much as they need water.”","He gave her a second tray and a thin wooden skewer. The rule was easy to remember. Push the skewer in, and if it comes out dark, skip the watering that day.","Elena watered the second tray four times in two weeks. On the ninth morning the first radish leaves came up, and she counted eleven of them before the bell rang."].join("\n\n");const GAUGE_TITLE="What a Rain Gauge Knows";const GAUGE=["A rain gauge is one of the simplest instruments in science and one of the oldest. It is a straight-sided tube with a scale printed up the side. Rain falls in, and you read how deep it is.","The shape matters more than it looks. The tube has to be the same width all the way up. A tube that flared out at the top would catch rain over a wider opening than the part you read, and every number would come out too high.","A gauge also has to sit in the open. A wall or a tree beside it blocks part of the rain and shelters the tube, so the reading comes out low. Weather services ask for a gauge to stand at least twice as far from the nearest tall object as that object is high.","Rain is measured in depth, not in bucketfuls. One inch of rain means that if none of it soaked in, ran off, or dried away, the ground would be covered one inch deep. One inch of rain on a single acre is a little over 27,000 gallons of water.","Read the gauge with your eye level with the water. Looking down at it from above makes the water line appear higher on the scale than it really is, and a reading taken that way is always too big."].join("\n\n");const gaugeHtml=small=>F.passage(GAUGE,GAUGE_TITLE,!!small);const ROOT_SRC="Source 1: Roots and Soil, a field guide for school gardens, by Dr. Amara Nwosu, 2023.";const ROOT_TITLE="The Bottom of the Plant";const ROOT=["A plant cannot go looking for water, so it grows toward it. Under every plant is a root system, and most of it is far finer than people expect.","The thick roots hold the plant down. Almost all of the water comes in somewhere else, through root hairs, which are single cells so thin you need a lens to see one. A single winter rye plant was once measured and found to carry about fourteen billion root hairs.","Root hairs pull in water along with the minerals dissolved in it. They cannot do that in soil that has been packed down or left soaked, because then there are no air pockets left. Roots need air as well as water, and a root with no air around it stops taking up water at all."].join("\n\n");const LEAF_SRC="Source 2: The Water Path, a plant science booklet by Felix Moreau, 2024.";const LEAF_TITLE="The Top of the Plant";const LEAF=["Most of the water a plant takes in does not stay in the plant. It travels up the stem and goes out through tiny openings on the underside of the leaves. Each opening is called a stoma, and a pair of guard cells can open it or close it.","When water vapor leaves through the stomata, it tugs the next bit of water up behind it, the way a sip pulls juice up a straw. That pull runs all the way down the stem to the roots.","A plant lets out far more water than it keeps. One corn plant can move several quarts of water through itself on a hot day. On a hot, windy afternoon the guard cells may close the stomata partway to slow the loss, and that slows the pull as well."].join("\n\n");const twoTexts=()=>`<div class="texts" style="display:flex;gap:18px;flex-wrap:wrap"><article style="flex:1 1 320px"><p class="src" style="margin:0 0 6px">${ROOT_SRC}</p>${F.passage(ROOT,ROOT_TITLE,true)}</article><article style="flex:1 1 320px"><p class="src" style="margin:0 0 6px">${LEAF_SRC}</p>${F.passage(LEAF,LEAF_TITLE,true)}</article></div>`;const PLAN_READ=["How the Greenhouse Runs.","The school greenhouse opens on the first of March and closes at the end of May. Three classes share it, and each class gets the whole space for two weeks at a time.","A class takes on three jobs while it has the greenhouse. One student reads the rain gauge each morning and writes the number on the chart. Two students check the thermometer at noon and open or close the roof vent. Everybody else waters, but only after the skewer test.","There is one rule nobody is allowed to break. No tray leaves the greenhouse until its plants have four true leaves, because a seedling moved outside too early will not take root."].join("\n\n");const WORDS=["transplant","germinate","moisture","evaporate","nutrient","disappear","unsteady","measurement","absorbent","carefully","seedling","thermometer"];const WRITE_DIR="Write on your paper. When you finish, hold it up or read it to your teacher.";const checklist=items=>`<ul class="qlist" style="margin-top:14px">${items.map(x=>`<li>${x}</li>`).join("")}</ul>`;const pills=(...w)=>`<div class="row center" style="margin-top:18px;gap:18px;flex-wrap:wrap">${w.map(x=>`<span class="pill" style="font-size:1.4rem">${x}</span>`).join("")}</div>`;const lookBack=(label,inner)=>`<details class="look-back" open><summary>Look back at ${label}</summary>${inner}</details>`;const group=(inner,label)=>`<div style="text-align:center">${inner}<p class="fig-label" style="margin:4px 0 0">${label}</p></div>`;const stackFig=inner=>`<div style="display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:12px;align-items:center;margin-top:10px">${inner}</div>`;function noteBoxes(a,b){const bx=t=>`<div style="border:3px dashed var(--line);border-radius:16px;min-width:250px;min-height:110px;padding:8px 16px;background:var(--surface)"><p class="fig-label" style="margin:0;font-size:1.3rem">${t}</p></div>`;return`<div class="figure">${bx(a)}${bx(b)}</div>`}const PXFT=30;function bedRect(w,h,label){const W=w*PXFT,H=h*PXFT,VW=W+96,VH=H+62;return`<svg viewBox="0 0 ${VW} ${VH}" width="${VW}" style="max-width:100%;height:auto" role="img" aria-label="${label}, a rectangle ${w} feet by ${h} feet drawn to scale"><rect x="52" y="10" width="${W}" height="${H}" fill="var(--sea-soft)" stroke="var(--fig-ink)" stroke-width="3"/><text x="${52+W/2}" y="${H+40}" font-size="19" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--ink)">${w} ft</text><text x="44" y="${10+H/2}" font-size="19" font-weight="700" text-anchor="end" dominant-baseline="central" fill="var(--ink)">${h} ft</text></svg>`}function blankTable(a,b,rows){let s=`<table class="chart" style="margin:0 auto"><tr><th style="text-align:center">${a}</th><th style="text-align:center">${b}</th></tr>`;rows.forEach(r=>{s+=`<tr><td style="text-align:center">${r}</td><td style="text-align:center">?</td></tr>`});return`<div class="tool-scroll">${s}</table></div>`}const LONGROW_TITLE="The Long Row";const LONGROW=["The bed by the fence had been under a tarp all winter, and when Theo pulled the tarp back the whole thing was a mat of roots and dead stalks.","Clearing it looked like a Herculean job. He worked at one end with a garden fork for an hour and barely moved a foot of it. His sister came out with a second fork at noon and started at the other end.","By four o'clock the bed was bare brown soil and Theo's arms felt like wet rope. “The hardest part is behind us,” his sister said, and he decided to believe her."].join("\n\n");function figSortFig(){const st=`stroke="var(--fig-ink)" stroke-width="2.8" stroke-linejoin="round"`,fill=`fill="var(--sea-soft)"`;const c=176,w=5*c+28,h=c+50;let b=`<rect x="3" y="3" width="${w-6}" height="${h-6}" rx="20" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--line)" stroke-width="4"/>`;for(let i=0;i<5;i++){b+=`<rect x="${14+i*c}" y="12" width="${c-16}" height="${c-16}" rx="16" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--line)" stroke-width="3"/>`;b+=`<text x="${14+i*c+(c-16)/2}" y="${h-16}" font-size="24" font-family="Andika,sans-serif" font-weight="700" fill="var(--muted)" text-anchor="middle">${i+1}</text>`}const g=(i,inner)=>`<g transform="translate(${14+i*c+18} 30) scale(1.24)">${inner}</g>`;const sq=(x,y,dx,dy)=>`<path d="M${x+12*dx} ${y} v${12*dy} h${-12*dx}" fill="none" stroke="var(--fig-ink)" stroke-width="2"/>`;b+=g(0,`<polygon points="22,86 22,24 88,86" ${fill} ${st}/>`+sq(22,86,1,-1));b+=g(1,`<polygon points="50,18 90,86 14,76" ${fill} ${st}/>`);b+=g(2,`<polygon points="14,86 74,86 94,24 34,24" ${fill} ${st}/>`);b+=g(3,`<rect x="10" y="28" width="80" height="54" ${fill} ${st}/>`+sq(10,82,1,-1));b+=g(4,`<polygon points="20,86 92,86 74,20 38,20" ${fill} ${st}/>`);return`<svg viewBox="0 0 ${w} ${h}" width="${Math.min(w,980)}" role="img" aria-label="Five numbered figures. Figure 1 is a triangle with a small square marking a right angle at its lower left corner. Figure 2 is a triangle with no right angle. Figure 3 is a slanted parallelogram. Figure 4 is a rectangle with a small square marking a right angle. Figure 5 is a trapezoid whose top and bottom sides are parallel and whose two slanted sides are not.">${b}</svg>`}const STOPS=[{id:1,title:"Greenhouse Word Cards",short:"Word Cards",parts:[{std:["RF.4.3"],kind:"words",words:WORDS,n:12,dur:75,dir:"Read each word out loud. Break it into parts you know, then put it back together.",desc:"reading unfamiliar multisyllabic words using syllable patterns and word parts",key:["Read each word in order: transplant, germinate, moisture, evaporate, nutrient, disappear, unsteady, measurement, absorbent, carefully, seedling, thermometer.","Word parts to listen for: trans- means across (transplant), dis- and un- flip the meaning (disappear, unsteady), -ment and -ing and -ly are endings added to a base (measurement, seedling, carefully), -ent describes (absorbent), and thermometer is built from therm, meaning heat, and meter, meaning measure.","Syllable work to watch: germinate and evaporate are four syllables with a long a in the last one, moisture ends in the -ture chunk that says cher, and nutrient has three syllables with the i and the e in separate ones.","Mark a word wrong only if the student cannot read it correctly after one try at chunking it."]}]},{id:2,title:"The Same Amount, Cut Differently",short:"Same Amount",parts:[{std:["4.NF.A.1"],dur:150,dir:"Answer out loud. For each one, tell how you know. You can write on your paper.",desc:"explaining why two fractions are equivalent using a model and using multiplication of the parts",html:()=>`<p class="big" style="margin-top:0">Two seed trays are exactly the same size. Tray A is split into 4 equal sections. Tray B is split into 12 equal sections. The shaded part of each one is already planted.</p>`,fig:()=>`<div class="figure" style="gap:26px;align-items:flex-end">${group(F.fracBar(4,1,{width:340}),"Tray A")}${group(F.fracBar(12,3,{width:340}),"Tray B")}</div>`,qs:["Is the planted part of Tray A the same amount as the planted part of Tray B? Write it as an equation with two fractions.","Use the picture to explain why that equation is true. What happened to each section of Tray A?","Write a fraction equal to 2/5 that has 10 as the bottom number. Show the multiplication you used.","Write a fraction equal to 6/8 that has a smaller bottom number. Show how you got it.","Explain why 2/3 and 8/12 are the same amount, without drawing.","Lior says 3/4 and 5/6 must be equal because the top and the bottom of each one are 1 apart. Is Lior right? Show why or why not."],key:["Yes. 1/4 = 3/12. Partial: saying yes with no equation written is approaching. (4.NF.A.1)","Each of the 4 sections of Tray A was cut into 3 smaller sections, which makes 12 sections in all. The one shaded section became 3 shaded sections. Nothing was added or taken away, so the amount is the same. In numbers, 1/4 = (1 × 3)/(4 × 3) = 3/12. Partial: naming the cutting with nothing said about the shaded section is approaching. (4.NF.A.1, explaining equivalence with a visual model)","4/10. Multiply the top and the bottom by the same number: (2 × 2)/(5 × 2) = 4/10. Partial: 4/10 right with no multiplication shown is approaching. (4.NF.A.1)","3/4. Divide the top and the bottom by the same number: (6 ÷ 2)/(8 ÷ 2) = 3/4. Partial: 3/4 right with no dividing shown is approaching. (4.NF.A.1)","Cutting each third into 4 equal pieces gives twelfths, and the 2 shaded thirds become 8 shaded twelfths. In numbers, (2 × 4)/(3 × 4) = 8/12. Partial: naming the cutting with no numbers given is approaching. (4.NF.A.1)","No. Equivalent fractions come from multiplying the top and the bottom by the same number, and there is no whole number that turns 3/4 into 5/6. In twelfths, 3/4 = 9/12 and 5/6 = 10/12, so 5/6 is larger. Partial: saying no with no comparison in twelfths is approaching. (4.NF.A.1)"]}]},{id:3,title:"Read the Rain Gauge",short:"Read Aloud",parts:[{std:["RF.4.4"],kind:"read",n:0,label:"words read correctly",dur:120,dir:"Read the passage out loud. Use your best reading voice.",desc:"a grade-level passage read aloud",html:()=>F.passage(FLU,FLU_TITLE),key:["__WC__ words, not counting the title. Time 1 minute and mark errors and self-corrections on your own copy. Let the student finish the passage.","Spring grade 4 benchmark: about 133 words correct per minute (Hasbrouck and Tindal 2017, spring 50th percentile). 25th percentile is about 105. This is the last check of the year, so the spring column is the right one.","Note pace, phrasing, and expression. Listen for the long second sentence of the middle paragraph, which needs a pause at each comma, and for the word gauge, which is often read as gage or guage."]}]},{id:4,title:"What a Rain Gauge Knows",short:"The Gauge",parts:[{std:["RI.4.1"],dur:125,dir:"Read the text. Then answer out loud. Point to the details in the text that back up each answer.",desc:"explaining what an informational text says and drawing inferences, with details from the text",html:()=>gaugeHtml(true),qs:["Why does the tube have to be the same width all the way up? Use the text.","How far from a tall object should a gauge stand? Read the words that tell you.","What does one inch of rain mean? Read the sentence that explains it.","How much water is one inch of rain on one acre?","The text never says that a gauge under a porch roof would be useless. Use details from the text to explain why it would be."],key:["Because a tube that flared out at the top would catch rain over a wider opening than the part you read, so every number would come out too high. (RI.4.1, explicit detail)","At least twice as far from the nearest tall object as that object is high. (RI.4.1, explicit detail)","Reads “if none of it soaked in, ran off, or dried away, the ground would be covered one inch deep.” (RI.4.1)","A little over 27,000 gallons. (RI.4.1, explicit detail)","A roof over the tube would block the rain completely, so the gauge would read near zero no matter how hard it rained. The details that lead there: a wall or a tree beside a gauge blocks part of the rain and makes the reading come out low, and a gauge has to sit in the open. (RI.4.1, inference backed by details)"]}]},{id:5,title:"Which Row Got More?",short:"Compare",parts:[{std:["4.NF.A.2"],dur:150,dir:"Answer out loud. Tell how you know. You can write on your paper.",desc:"comparing fractions with different numerators and denominators, by common denominators and by benchmarks",html:()=>`<p class="big" style="margin-top:0">The crew writes down what fraction of each row has come up. Every row in the greenhouse is the same length.</p>`,fig:()=>`<div class="figure" style="gap:24px;align-items:flex-end">${group(F.fracBar(5,3,{width:300}),"Row A: 3/5 up")}${group(F.fracBar(3,2,{width:300}),"Row B: 2/3 up")}</div>`,qs:["Which is greater, 3/5 or 2/3? Show how you decided.","Which is greater, 5/8 or 3/4?","Use 1/2 as a benchmark. Is 4/9 of a row more or less than half a row?","Put these in order from least to greatest: 2/3, 1/4, 5/6, 1/2.","Which is greater, 7/10 or 3/5?","Row C is in a different greenhouse and is much longer. Bo says 1/2 of Row C is the same amount as 1/2 of Row A because both are one half. Is that right? Tell why or why not."],key:["2/3. In fifteenths, 3/5 = 9/15 and 2/3 = 10/15, so 2/3 is greater. The picture agrees. Partial: naming 2/3 with no common denominator shown is approaching. (4.NF.A.2, common denominator)","3/4. In eighths, 3/4 = 6/8, and 6/8 is more than 5/8. Partial: naming 3/4 with no common denominator shown is approaching. (4.NF.A.2)","Less than half. Half of 9 is 4 1/2, and 4 is below that. In eighteenths, 4/9 = 8/18 and 1/2 = 9/18. Partial: saying less with no benchmark work shown is approaching. (4.NF.A.2, benchmark)","1/4, 1/2, 2/3, 5/6. In twelfths they are 3/12, 6/12, 8/12, 10/12. Partial: three of the four in order is approaching. (4.NF.A.2, ordering)","7/10. In tenths, 3/5 = 6/10, and 7/10 is more than 6/10. Partial: naming 7/10 with no common denominator shown is approaching. (4.NF.A.2)","No. Two fractions can only be compared when they refer to the same whole. Row C is longer, so half of Row C is a bigger amount than half of Row A, even though both are written 1/2. Partial: saying no with nothing said about the same whole is approaching. (4.NF.A.2, comparisons are valid only for the same whole)"]}]},{id:6,title:"The Second Tray",short:"The Tray",parts:[{std:["RL.4.1"],dur:125,dir:"Read the story. Then answer out loud. Use details and examples from the story in every answer.",desc:"explaining what a story says and drawing inferences, using details and examples from the text",html:()=>F.passage(STORY,STORY_TITLE,true),qs:["What was different about Elena's tray at the end of the second week? Use details from the story.","The story never says Elena was trying hard. Which details show that she was?","What does Mr. Haddad mean when he says “A seed is not a fish”? Use a detail from the story.","How do you know the skewer rule worked? Give two details.","Why did Mr. Haddad push his finger into the soil before he said anything? Use a detail from the story."],key:["Her tray had a dull green crust on top of the soil and nothing growing, while the other trays had small green rows across them. (RL.4.1, explicit details)","She planted on the same day as everybody else, she watered every day and twice a day, and the story says she did that “because she wanted to be sure.” (RL.4.1, inference with details)","A seed does not live in water. He says it right after the next line: “Roots need air as much as they need water,” and his finger came up wet and dark, which showed the soil had no air left in it. (RL.4.1, inference with a detail)","She watered the second tray only four times in two weeks, and on the ninth morning the first radish leaves came up and she counted eleven of them. (RL.4.1, two details)","He was checking how wet the soil was before he guessed at the cause. The detail is that it came up wet and dark, which answered the question for him. Accept that he wanted Elena to see and feel it herself, backed by “held it out to her.” (RL.4.1, inference with a detail)"]}]},{id:7,title:"Big Units and Small Units",short:"Units",parts:[{std:["4.MD.A.1"],dur:150,dir:"Answer out loud. For number 4, copy the table onto your paper, fill it in, and hold it up.",desc:"knowing the relative size of measurement units and changing a larger unit into a smaller one, including in a two-column table",html:()=>`<p class="big" style="margin-top:0">The greenhouse order form only accepts the smaller unit. Change each measurement before it goes on the form.</p>`,fig:()=>`<div class="figure">${blankTable("Feet","Inches",[1,2,3,4,5])}</div>`,qs:["The potting bench is 3 meters long. How many centimeters is that?","A watering can holds 4 liters. How many milliliters is that?","A bag of potting soil weighs 5 kilograms. How many grams is that?","Copy the Feet and Inches table and fill in the Inches column for 1, 2, 3, 4, and 5 feet.","A watering shift lasts 2 hours. How many minutes is that? How many seconds?","One seed tray is 45 centimeters long. Two trays end to end make how many centimeters. Is that more or less than 1 meter?"],key:["300 centimeters. 1 meter is 100 centimeters, so 3 × 100 = 300. Partial: 30 uses tens instead of hundreds and does not count. (4.MD.A.1)","4,000 milliliters. 1 liter is 1,000 milliliters, so 4 × 1,000 = 4,000. Partial: 400 uses hundreds instead of thousands and does not count. (4.MD.A.1)","5,000 grams. 1 kilogram is 1,000 grams, so 5 × 1,000 = 5,000. Partial: 500 uses hundreds instead of thousands and does not count. (4.MD.A.1)","The Inches column reads 12, 24, 36, 48, 60, because 1 foot is 12 inches. Every row has to be filled, and the numbers must line up with 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 feet in order. Partial: four of the five rows right is approaching. (4.MD.A.1, a two-column table of equivalent measurements)","120 minutes and 7,200 seconds. 2 × 60 = 120 minutes, and 120 × 60 = 7,200 seconds. Partial: the minutes right with no seconds given is approaching. (4.MD.A.1)","90 centimeters, and that is less than 1 meter, because 1 meter is 100 centimeters and 90 is under 100. Partial: 90 right with no comparison to 1 meter is approaching. (4.MD.A.1, relative size of units)"]}]},{id:8,title:"Beds and Edging",short:"Area",parts:[{std:["4.MD.A.3"],dur:160,dir:"Use the two pictures for numbers 5. Work the rest on your paper and hold it up. Say the unit in every answer.",desc:"applying the area and perimeter formulas for rectangles in real-world problems",html:()=>`<p class="big" style="margin-top:0">Edging is the wooden strip that runs all the way around the outside of a raised bed. Beds D and E below are drawn to the same scale.</p>`,fig:()=>`<div class="figure" style="gap:30px;align-items:flex-end">${group(bedRect(6,5,"Bed D"),"Bed D")}${group(bedRect(7,4,"Bed E"),"Bed E")}</div>`,qs:["A raised bed is 8 feet long and 3 feet wide. What is its area? What is its perimeter?","The greenhouse floor is 24 feet by 12 feet. What is its area?","A bed has an area of 40 square feet and one side is 5 feet long. How long is the other side, and what is the perimeter?","Edging has to run all the way around a bed that is 9 feet by 4 feet. The edging comes in 2 foot pieces. How many pieces are needed?","Look at Bed D and Bed E. Which one has more growing area? Which one needs more edging?","A seed tray is 18 inches by 11 inches. What is its area, and what is its perimeter?"],key:["Area 24 square feet, from 8 × 3. Perimeter 22 feet, from 2 × (8 + 3). The area must be in square feet and the perimeter in plain feet. Partial: one of the area and the perimeter right is approaching. (4.MD.A.3)","288 square feet, from 24 × 12. Partial: 288 with no square unit named is approaching. (4.MD.A.3)","The other side is 8 feet, because 40 ÷ 5 = 8. The perimeter is 26 feet, from 2 × (5 + 8). Partial: the side right with no perimeter given is approaching. (4.MD.A.3, area formula with an unknown side)","13 pieces. The perimeter is 2 × (9 + 4) = 26 feet, and 26 ÷ 2 = 13. Partial: the perimeter right with no division done is approaching. (4.MD.A.3)","Bed D has more growing area: 6 × 5 = 30 square feet against 7 × 4 = 28 square feet. Neither needs more edging, because both perimeters are 22 feet: 2 × (6 + 5) = 22 and 2 × (7 + 4) = 22. Two rectangles can have the same perimeter and different areas. Partial: the area question answered with the edging question left out is approaching. (4.MD.A.3)","Area 198 square inches, from 18 × 11. Perimeter 58 inches, from 2 × (18 + 11). Partial: one of the area and the perimeter right is approaching. (4.MD.A.3)"]}]},{id:9,title:"Roots and Leaves",short:"Two Texts",parts:[{std:["RI.4.9"],dur:140,dir:"Read both sources. They are about the same subject. Then answer out loud, using both of them.",desc:"putting information from two texts on one topic together in order to speak about the subject knowledgeably",html:twoTexts,qs:["Use both sources to describe the whole path water takes, from the soil to the air.","Source 2 says the water vapor leaving the leaves tugs the next water up behind it. Use Source 1 to say what is doing the taking in at the bottom of that pull.","Each source names something that can stop water from moving. What does each one name?","Put the two sources together. Why might a plant wilt on a hot afternoon even when the soil around it is wet?","In two or three sentences, explain how a plant drinks. Use at least one fact from each source."],key:["Water in the soil is taken in by root hairs along with the minerals dissolved in it (Source 1), travels up the stem, and goes out as water vapor through the stomata on the underside of the leaves (Source 2). Needs the path in order and a fact from each source. (RI.4.9, integrating two texts)","Root hairs, which are single cells so thin you need a lens to see one, and which pull in the water along with the dissolved minerals. (RI.4.9)","Source 1: soil packed down or left soaked has no air pockets, and a root with no air around it stops taking up water. Source 2: on a hot, windy afternoon the guard cells can close the stomata partway, which slows the loss and slows the pull. (RI.4.9, a point from each text)","The leaves can lose water faster than the roots can replace it, because a corn plant can move several quarts through itself on a hot day. And if the soil is wet because it is soaked solid, there is no air in it, so the roots are not taking water up at all. Either reason earns the check if both sources are used. (RI.4.9, integrating to answer a new question)","Any clear two or three sentences that use at least one fact from each source, such as root hairs taking in water and minerals, the stomata letting vapor out, the straw-like pull up the stem, or roots needing air. (RI.4.9, speaking about the subject knowledgeably)"]}]},{id:10,title:"Fix the Greenhouse Log",short:"Fix It",parts:[{title:"One Mistake Each",std:["L.4.1"],dur:80,dir:"Each line from the greenhouse log has one mistake. Say the whole line out loud the right way.",desc:"fixing spoken grammar mistakes (modal auxiliaries, adjective order, confused words, relative pronouns, fragments)",qs:["We should of opened the roof vent at noon.","Put the two plastic big trays by the door.","Their are six trays on the top shelf.","The gauge, which it stands on the post, was full this morning.","Because the soil was still dark."],key:["We should have opened the roof vent at noon. (L.4.1c, modal auxiliary; “should of” is not a form)","Put the two big plastic trays by the door. Number, then size, then what it is made of, then the noun. (L.4.1d, ordering adjectives)","There are six trays on the top shelf. (L.4.1g, frequently confused words there, their, they're)","The gauge, which stands on the post, was full this morning. The relative pronoun which is already the subject, so the extra it has to go. (L.4.1a, relative pronouns)","Any complete sentence that uses the clause, such as: She skipped the watering because the soil was still dark. On its own it is a fragment, because it has no main clause. (L.4.1f, correcting fragments)"]},{title:"Build a Line",std:["L.4.1"],dur:60,dir:"Answer out loud. Say whole sentences.",desc:"spoken grammar items (relative adverbs, progressive tense, joining with a relative pronoun, confused words)",qs:["Which word fits? The greenhouse is the place ____ we start the seeds. Is it where, when, or why?","Change this sentence so it tells what is happening right now: Elena fills the watering can.","Join these into one sentence using that: Mr. Haddad gave her a skewer. The skewer tells you whether the soil is wet.","Which word fits? The tray was ____ wet to plant in. Is it to, too, or two?"],key:["where (L.4.1a, relative adverb)","Elena is filling the watering can. (L.4.1b, present progressive)","Mr. Haddad gave her a skewer that tells you whether the soil is wet. (L.4.1a, relative pronoun joining two sentences)","too (L.4.1g, frequently confused words to, too, two)"]}]},{id:11,title:"Say It Back",short:"Paraphrase",parts:[{std:["SL.4.2"],n:5,label:"checks met",dur:140,dir:"Listen while your teacher reads. Then say each part back in your own words. For the last two, use the graph.",desc:"paraphrasing a text read aloud and information shown on a graph",read:PLAN_READ,html:()=>`<p class="big" style="margin-top:0">Your teacher will read you a short notice about the greenhouse. Listen carefully. Then say each part back in your own words, not in the words your teacher used. After that, look at the graph.</p>`,fig:()=>F.row([F.barGraph({title:"Rain this spring",cats:["March","April","May"],vals:[4,6,2],max:8,step:1,yLabel:"inches"}),"Inches of rain in the gauge, month by month"]),qs:["Say the first part back in your own words: when the greenhouse is open and who uses it.","Say the three jobs back in your own words.","What is the one rule, and what reason was given for it? Put both in your own words.","Look at the graph. Say in your own words what it shows.","Which month had the most rain. How much more than the driest month on the graph?"],key:["The greenhouse is open for three months in spring, from the start of March to the end of May, and three classes take turns, two weeks each. Accept any wording that keeps both facts. If the student repeats the sentence word for word, that is not a paraphrase. (SL.4.2, paraphrasing a text read aloud)","One person reads the rain gauge in the morning and records it; two people check the temperature at midday and work the roof vent; everyone else waters, but only when the skewer says the soil is dry. Needs all three jobs in the student's own words. (SL.4.2)","The rule: a tray cannot leave the greenhouse until its plants have four true leaves. The reason: a seedling taken outside too soon will not take root. Both parts, in the student's own words. (SL.4.2)","The graph shows how much rain fell each month this spring, measured in inches. April was the wettest month and May was the driest. Accept any correct paraphrase of what the graph is about. (SL.4.2, information presented visually and quantitatively)","April had the most, with 6 inches. May was the driest, with 2 inches. April had 4 inches more than May. (SL.4.2, quantitative information)"]}]},{id:12,title:"Two Sources, Two Boxes",short:"Notes",parts:[{std:["W.4.8"],n:4,label:"checks met",dur:110,dir:"Read both sources. Take your notes on your paper, then list your sources at the bottom. Hold it up when you finish.",desc:"taking notes from two sources, sorting them into categories, and listing the sources",html:()=>`${twoTexts()}<p class="big">Make two boxes on your paper and label them like the boxes below. Write at least two short notes in each box. Under the boxes, write a list of your sources.</p>${noteBoxes("How water gets in","How water gets out")}<p class="small" style="text-align:center;font-size:1.2rem">A note is a few words, not a whole sentence. A source line names the title and who wrote it.</p>`,key:["The “gets in” box has at least two notes from Source 1, such as root hairs take in water, single cells, need a lens to see, minerals dissolved in the water, roots need air, packed or soaked soil stops it. (W.4.8, categorizing information)","The “gets out” box has at least two notes from Source 2, such as water goes out through stomata on the underside of leaves, guard cells open and close them, vapor leaving pulls the next water up like a straw, a corn plant moves several quarts on a hot day. (W.4.8, categorizing information)","The notes are brief: a few words or a phrase each, not sentences copied whole out of the source. (W.4.8, taking notes)","A source list with both sources, each naming the title and the author: Roots and Soil by Dr. Amara Nwosu, and The Water Path by Felix Moreau. The year counts as a bonus, not a requirement. (W.4.8, providing a list of sources)"]}]},{id:13,title:"Greenhouse Writer",short:"Write",parts:[{alt:{group:"write",key:"opinion",label:"Opinion",icon:"💬",hint:"Share it, or give it to one class?"},std:["W.4.1"],n:5,label:"checks met",dur:200,dir:WRITE_DIR,desc:"an opinion writing piece",html:()=>`<p class="big">Right now three classes share the greenhouse, two weeks at a time. Some people want to change that and give the whole greenhouse to one class for the whole spring instead. Sharing means every class gets a turn, but a tray often has to move out before its plants have four true leaves. One class for the whole spring means nothing has to move early, but two classes get no greenhouse at all.</p><p class="big">Which way should the school run it? Write your opinion with reasons. Write 6 to 8 sentences.</p>${checklist(["Open by naming the topic and stating your opinion clearly","Give reasons that are backed by facts and details","Link your opinion and reasons with words such as because, for instance, in order to, also","End with a conclusion that follows from your reasons"])}`,key:["Introduces the topic and states an opinion clearly (W.4.1a)","Groups the reasons so they build on each other instead of jumping around (W.4.1a)","Supports each reason with facts and details, such as the two-week turn, the four true leaves rule, or the two classes that would get nothing (W.4.1b)","Links the opinion and the reasons with words and phrases such as because, for instance, in order to, also (W.4.1c)","Ends with a concluding statement that follows from the opinion presented (W.4.1d)"]},{alt:{group:"write",key:"facts",label:"Explain",icon:"📘",hint:"Teach how a plant drinks"},std:["W.4.2"],n:5,label:"checks met",dur:200,dir:WRITE_DIR,desc:"an informative writing piece",html:()=>`<p class="big">Write to teach a third grader how water travels through a plant, from the soil to the air. Use facts from both sources. Write 6 to 8 sentences.</p>${checklist(["Open by naming your topic","Group the facts so the path is in order, from the roots to the leaves","Use facts, definitions, and details from the sources","Use linking words such as then, after that, because, for example","Use the words a plant scientist would use, such as root hairs and stomata, and tell the reader what they mean","End with a closing sentence"])}${lookBack("the two sources",twoTexts())}`,key:["Introduces the topic clearly and groups related information together (W.4.2a)","Develops the topic with facts, definitions, and concrete details from the sources (W.4.2b)","The path is in an order a reader can follow, from soil to root hairs to stem to stomata (W.4.2a)","Links ideas with words and phrases such as then, after that, because, for example (W.4.2c)","Uses precise language and subject vocabulary, such as root hairs, stomata, guard cells, or water vapor, and ends with a concluding statement (W.4.2d and W.4.2e)"]},{alt:{group:"write",key:"story",label:"Story",icon:"🌱",hint:"The morning the roof vent stuck"},std:["W.4.3"],n:5,label:"checks met",dur:200,dir:WRITE_DIR,desc:"a narrative writing piece",html:()=>`<p class="big">It is the warmest morning of the spring. You get to the greenhouse first and find the roof vent stuck shut and the thermometer already higher than you have ever seen it. Write the story of the next twenty minutes. Write 8 to 10 sentences.</p>${pills("At first","Then","After that","By the time","In the end")}${checklist(["Set up the situation and say who is in the story","Tell the events in an order that makes sense","Use dialogue and describe what the characters do and feel","Use transition words to show time moving","End with something that follows from what happened"])}`,key:["Orients the reader by setting up the situation and introducing the narrator and any other characters (W.4.3a)","Organizes the events in a sequence that unfolds naturally (W.4.3a)","Uses dialogue and description to show how the characters respond (W.4.3b)","Uses transitional words and phrases to manage the order of events (W.4.3c)","Uses concrete words and sensory detail, and ends with a conclusion that follows from the events (W.4.3d and W.4.3e)"]}]},{id:14,title:"What the Words Mean",short:"Word Meaning",parts:[{std:["RL.4.4"],n:3,dur:55,dir:"Read the passage. Then answer out loud.",desc:"working out the meaning of words and phrases in a story, including a word that comes from an old story",html:()=>F.passage(LONGROW,LONGROW_TITLE),qs:["The passage calls clearing the bed a Herculean job. What does Herculean mean here, and what in the passage helped you work it out?","Theo's arms felt like wet rope. What does that tell you about how he felt?","His sister says the hardest part is behind us. Is anything really behind them? What does the phrase mean?"],key:["Enormously hard, needing great strength or effort. The word comes from Hercules, a hero in old Greek stories who was famous for his strength. The clues in the passage are the mat of roots and dead stalks, an hour of work for barely a foot of bed, and needing a second person with a second fork. Not knowing where the word comes from is fine as long as the meaning is right","That his arms had gone limp and had no strength left in them, because he was worn out. It is a comparison, not a description of what his arms were made of","Nothing is really behind them. Behind us means in the past, already finished. She means the worst of the work is over and the rest will be easier"]}]},{id:15,title:"Tell It Yourself",short:"Tell It",parts:[{std:["SL.4.4"],n:4,label:"checks met",dur:65,dir:"Think for a moment. Then tell your teacher out loud. Talk for about a minute. Speak clearly and not too fast.",desc:"recounting an experience in an organized way, with facts and descriptive details, at an understandable pace",html:()=>`<p class="big" style="margin-top:0">Tell about one time you grew something, or looked after a plant or an animal. Start at the beginning and take it through to how it turned out.</p>${pills("What it was","What you did, in order","A detail a listener can picture","How it turned out")}`,key:["Recounts one experience and stays on it for about a minute, rather than jumping between several (SL.4.4)","The account is organized: it opens by saying what the thing was, moves through what happened in an order a listener can follow, and closes instead of trailing off (SL.4.4, organized manner)","Uses appropriate facts, the real particulars of it: what it was, how long it took, how often it was watered or fed, what finally happened (SL.4.4, appropriate facts)","Adds at least one relevant descriptive detail a listener can picture, and speaks clearly at a pace you can follow, without rushing (SL.4.4)"]}]},{id:16,title:"Times As Many",short:"Times As",parts:[{std:["4.OA.A.1"],n:3,dur:50,dir:"Write each equation on your paper. Say the comparison out loud.",desc:"reading a multiplication equation as a comparison and writing a comparison as an equation",html:()=>`<p class="big" style="margin-top:0">The greenhouse counts 42 radish seedlings and 7 lettuce seedlings.</p>`,qs:["Write a multiplication equation that compares those two counts. Then say what it means in words.","Say the same equation the other way round, starting with the 7.","Write this sentence as an equation and solve it: the tall sunflower is 5 times as tall as the short one. The short one is 9 inches."],key:["42 = 6 x 7, or 6 x 7 = 42. In words: 42 is 6 times as many as 7. Listen for times as many, not 6 more than 7. Partial: the equation right with no words given is approaching.","42 is 7 times as many as 6. The same equation says both comparisons, and noticing that is the point of this one. Partial: 42 is 7 more than 6 reads it as an addition and does not count.","t = 5 x 9, so t = 45 inches. Writing 45 = 5 x 9 is the same statement. A student who writes 9 + 5 has read it as an additive comparison instead of a multiplicative one. Partial: the equation right with no height found is approaching."]}]},{id:17,title:"Ten Times the Place",short:"Ten Times",parts:[{std:["4.NBT.A.1"],n:3,dur:45,dir:"Answer out loud. You can write on your paper.",desc:"knowing that a digit is worth ten times what the same digit is worth one place to its right",html:()=>`<p class="big" style="margin-top:0">The greenhouse has started 4,400 seeds since the first of March.</p>`,qs:["In 4,400 the digit 4 appears twice. What is each 4 worth, and how do the two values compare?","Explain why 700 divided by 70 is 10, using place value.","A number has a 6 in the thousands place. What would a 6 in the place just to its right be worth. How many of those make the thousands 6?"],key:["The left 4 is worth 4,000 and the right 4 is worth 400. 4,000 is ten times 400. Partial: the two values right with no comparison is approaching.","700 is 7 hundreds and 70 is 7 tens. One hundred is ten times one ten, so 7 hundreds is ten times 7 tens. That means 700 holds exactly ten 70s, so 700 / 70 = 10. Partial: working the division out with no place value reason is approaching.","A 6 in the hundreds place is worth 600. It takes ten 600s to make 6,000, so the thousands 6 is ten times the hundreds 6. Partial: 600 right with no count of how many make 6,000 is approaching."]}]},{id:18,title:"Round the Seed Count",short:"Rounding",parts:[{std:["4.NBT.A.3"],n:3,dur:45,dir:"Answer out loud. You can write on your paper.",desc:"rounding a multi-digit whole number to the thousand, the ten thousand, and the hundred",html:()=>`<p class="big" style="margin-top:0">A seed company says it shipped 27,486 seed packets this spring.</p>`,qs:["Round 27,486 to the nearest thousand. Then round it to the nearest ten thousand.","Round 27,486 to the nearest hundred.","A whole number rounds to 5,000 when it is rounded to the nearest thousand. What is the smallest it could be, and the largest?"],key:["27,000 to the nearest thousand, because the hundreds digit is 4, which is less than 5. 30,000 to the nearest ten thousand, because the thousands digit is 7, which is 5 or more. Partial: one of the two roundings right is approaching.","27,500, because the tens digit is 8, which is 5 or more, so the hundreds digit goes up from 4 to 5. Partial: 27,400 rounds down when the tens digit is 8 and does not count.","4,500 is the smallest and 5,499 is the largest. 4,499 would round to 4,000 and 5,500 would round to 6,000. Partial: one of the smallest and the largest given is approaching."]}]},{id:19,title:"Grids and Decimals",short:"Decimals",parts:[{std:["4.NF.C.6","4.NF.C.7"],n:3,dur:55,dir:"Each grid is one whole, cut into 100 equal squares. Write your answers on your paper.",desc:"writing hundredths as fractions and decimals, and comparing two decimals with a symbol",html:()=>`<p class="big" style="margin-top:0">Two garden plots are drawn as grids. Each grid is one whole plot, cut into 100 equal squares. The shaded squares are the part already planted.</p>`,fig:()=>F.row(group(F.gridRect(10,10,62,{cell:22}),"Grid A"),group(F.gridRect(10,10,70,{cell:22}),"Grid B")),qs:["Grid A has 62 of its 100 squares shaded. Write that as a fraction and as a decimal. Then say roughly where that decimal sits on a number line from 0 to 1.","Grid B is seven tenths shaded. Write seven tenths as a fraction with a denominator of 10, as a decimal, and as hundredths.","Compare the two decimals using one of the symbols >, = or <. Justify your answer with the grids."],key:["62/100 and 0.62 (4.NF.C.6). On a 0 to 1 line it sits between 0.6 and 0.7, a little past 0.6 and much nearer to it than to 0.7. Partial: the fraction and the decimal right with no place on the line is approaching.","7/10, 0.7, and 70/100 which is written 0.70 (4.NF.C.6). 0.7 and 0.70 are the same number. A student who writes 0.07 has put the 7 in the hundredths place. Partial: two of the three forms given is approaching.","0.62 < 0.7, which can also be written 0.7 > 0.62 (4.NF.C.7). Grid B has 70 shaded squares and Grid A has 62, and 70 hundredths is more than 62 hundredths. This comparison only works because the two grids are the same size whole. If Grid B were a much smaller plot, 0.7 of it could be less actual ground than 0.62 of Grid A. Partial: the right choice with the sign the wrong way round is approaching."]}]},{id:20,title:"Sort the Figures",short:"Figures",parts:[{std:["4.G.A.2"],n:3,dur:50,dir:"Look at the five figures. Answer out loud.",desc:"sorting figures by parallel sides, by perpendicular sides, and picking out the right triangle",fig:()=>F.row(figSortFig()),qs:["Which figures have at least one pair of parallel sides? Which one has exactly one pair?","Which figures have perpendicular sides, that is two sides that meet at a right angle?","Which figure is a right triangle, and how do you know?"],key:["Figures 3, 4 and 5 have parallel sides. Figure 5, the trapezoid, has exactly one pair: its top and bottom are parallel and its two slanted sides are not. Figures 3 and 4 each have two pairs. Figures 1 and 2 are triangles, and a triangle can never have parallel sides. Partial: the three figures named with no account of which has exactly one pair is approaching.","Figure 4, the rectangle, where every corner is a right angle, and figure 1, whose upright side and bottom side meet at a right angle. The small square drawn in the corner marks it in both. Partial: one of the two figures named is approaching.","Figure 1. It is a triangle with one angle of exactly 90 degrees, marked by the small square in its lower left corner. Figure 2 is a triangle too, but none of its angles is a right angle. Partial: naming Figure 1 with no reason is approaching."]}]},{id:21,title:"Flower and Frog",short:"Structures",parts:[{std:["4-LS1-1"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Look at the two living things. Answer out loud or in writing.",desc:"arguing that named plant and animal structures do a job that supports survival, growth or reproduction",html:()=>`<p class="big">A spring wildflower is open beside the pond. A frog sits on a stone at the edge.</p>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("tulip",104)}${F.icon("frog",104)}</div>
     <p class="big">The flower has bright petals, a store of nectar at its base, and a bulb underground. The frog has long back legs, webbed feet, eyes set high on its head, and lungs plus a damp skin that also takes in oxygen.</p>`,qs:["Name the job of the flower's bright petals and the job of the nectar.","Build an argument that the frog's eyes being set high on its head suits where it lives.","The flower's bulb is underground. What does it do for the plant, and is it an inside or an outside structure?"],key:["Bright petals attract insects from a distance, which brings pollen from flower to flower, so they support reproduction. Nectar is the reward that makes an insect come in and stay long enough to pick up and drop off pollen. (4-LS1-1)","A frog sits mostly in water with just the top of its head above the surface. Eyes set high mean it can see all round above the water while the rest of its body stays hidden and wet. Look for the claim plus reasoning from where it lives. (4-LS1-1)","The bulb stores food through the winter so the plant can grow quickly in spring, and it keeps the plant alive underground when the leaves above have died back. It is an outside structure of the whole plant but is underground: the point is that it is a storage structure. (4-LS1-1)"]},{std:["4-LS1-2"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Read what happens, then answer. You may draw your model.",desc:"modelling the path from sense organ to brain to response in an animal",html:()=>`<p class="big">A frog sits still on a stone. A shadow passes over the water as a heron flies by overhead. The frog pushes off at once and dives under the surface. A moment later it comes back up at the far side.</p>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("frog",104)}${F.icon("bluebird",104)}</div>`,qs:["Which sense took in the information, and which body part took it in?","What did the frog's brain do between seeing the shadow and pushing off?","Build a three step model of the path. It starts with the shadow on the water. It ends with the frog surfacing on the far side."],key:["Sight, taken in by the eyes. The information was the change in light, the shadow moving over the water. (4-LS1-2)","It processed what the eyes sent, worked out what a moving shadow overhead means, and chose a response out of several. All of that happened very quickly, but the brain is still the place it happened. (4-LS1-2)","The eyes take in the shadow, the brain processes it and chooses to dive, the back legs push off and the frog swims. A model without the brain step is approaching, not proficient. (4-LS1-2)"]}]},{id:22,title:"The Riverbank After the Melt",short:"Rock Layers",parts:[{std:["4-ESS1-1"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Study the bank. Answer out loud or in writing.",desc:"reading rock layers and the fossils in them as evidence of landscape change",html:()=>`<p class="big">Spring meltwater cut a new face into the riverbank and showed four layers. Layers stack with the newest on top.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Layer</th><th>What the layer is</th><th>Fossils in it</th></tr>
     <tr><td>top</td><td>sandy loam</td><td>grass roots and wildflower seeds</td></tr>
     <tr><td>second</td><td>volcanic ash, pale and gritty</td><td>charred wood and leaf prints</td></tr>
     <tr><td>third</td><td>brown siltstone</td><td>oak leaves and land snails</td></tr>
     <tr><td>bottom</td><td>limestone</td><td>coral and sea lilies</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Which layer formed first, and how does the chart show that?","Coral grows in warm shallow seas. What does the bottom layer tell you about this place long ago?","Use all four layers in order to explain how this landscape changed."],key:["The limestone at the bottom, because layers build upward and everything above it landed later. (4-ESS1-1)","A warm shallow sea covered this spot, warm enough and shallow enough for coral and sea lilies to live there. (4-ESS1-1)","A warm shallow sea left limestone with coral, then the sea gave way to land with oak woods and land snails in silt, then a volcano dropped ash over that land, then soil built up and meadow grew. Any account reading the chart upward and naming the changes counts. (4-ESS1-1)"]},{std:["4-ESS2-1"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Look at the measurements. Answer out loud or in writing.",desc:"using measurements to compare the rate of erosion by water, ice, wind or vegetation",html:()=>`<p class="big">A class measured how much the riverbank moved back at four places over one spring, using stakes set a measured distance from the edge.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Place</th><th>What the bank is like there</th><th>Bank lost over the spring</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1</td><td>bare soil on the outside of a bend</td><td>62 cm</td></tr>
     <tr><td>2</td><td>bare soil on a straight stretch</td><td>21 cm</td></tr>
     <tr><td>3</td><td>willow roots along the outside of a bend</td><td>9 cm</td></tr>
     <tr><td>4</td><td>large rock placed along a straight stretch</td><td>2 cm</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Which pair of places shows what the shape of the river does? Give the numbers and what they show.","Compare place 1 and place 3. What did the willow roots do?","Which place lost the least, and name one drawback of doing that along the whole river."],key:["Places 1 and 2, both bare soil: 62 cm on the outside of a bend and 21 cm on a straight stretch. Water moves fastest on the outside of a bend, so it wears that bank back three times as fast. (4-ESS2-1)","They cut the loss from 62 cm to 9 cm in the same kind of spot. Roots bind the soil together and hold it against the fast water, and the stems slow the water near the bank. (4-ESS2-1)","Place 4, the large rock, 2 cm. A drawback: rock is expensive and heavy to place, it gives no shade or food for wildlife, and armoring one stretch can push the fast water onto the next unprotected bank instead. (4-ESS2-1)"]}]},{id:23,title:"Rain Across the State",short:"Map Patterns",parts:[{std:["4-ESS2-2"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Study the rainfall chart. Answer out loud or in writing.",desc:"interpreting map data to describe patterns in Earth's features",html:()=>`<p class="big">A rainfall map gives the yearly rain at six stations along a line running inland from the ocean, across a mountain range, and out the other side.</p>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("rain",80)}${F.icon("mountain",80)}${F.icon("cactus",80)}</div>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Station</th><th>Height</th><th>Rain in a year</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1 at the coast</td><td>10 m</td><td>58 cm</td></tr>
     <tr><td>2 coastal hills</td><td>600 m</td><td>95 cm</td></tr>
     <tr><td>3 inland valley</td><td>120 m</td><td>34 cm</td></tr>
     <tr><td>4 west slope of the range</td><td>1,500 m</td><td>130 cm</td></tr>
     <tr><td>5 crest of the range</td><td>2,400 m</td><td>145 cm</td></tr>
     <tr><td>6 east of the range</td><td>1,300 m</td><td>18 cm</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Describe the pattern linking height and rainfall for stations 1, 2, 4 and 5.","Station 6 is high but very dry. Describe that with the numbers and name the pattern it belongs to.","A new station is placed at 1,900 m on the west slope. Predict its rainfall and explain your reasoning."],key:["Rising ground brings more rain. Rain goes up with height: 58 cm at 10 meters, 95 cm at 600 meters, 130 cm at 1,500 meters and 145 cm at the 2,400 meter crest. (4-ESS2-2)","Station 6 is at 1,300 meters but gets only 18 cm, far less than station 2 at half that height. It sits on the far side of the range, where air that has already dropped its rain coming over the crest arrives dry. That is a rain shadow. (4-ESS2-2)","Between 130 and 145 cm, so about 135 to 140 cm, because it sits between stations 4 and 5 on the same slope. The reasoning from the pattern matters more than the figure. (4-ESS2-2)"]},{std:["4-ESS3-2"],n:3,dur:95,dir:"Read the two plans. Answer out loud or in writing.",desc:"generating and comparing solutions that reduce the impact of a natural Earth process",html:()=>`<p class="big">Every spring the melt swells the river and water comes over the bank into the fields and the lowest street of the town. The county can fund one plan.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Plan</th><th>What it does</th><th>Cost</th><th>What else it changes</th></tr>
     <tr><td>A: build the levee higher along the town</td><td>holds a bigger flow inside the channel past the town</td><td>high</td><td>sends the same water faster to the next town downstream</td></tr>
     <tr><td>B: move the levee back and let the low fields flood on purpose</td><td>gives the water room to spread and slow down before it reaches the town</td><td>medium, and the fields are lost in wet years</td><td>wetland returns, and the water arrives downstream later and lower</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Name a third plan of your own for the spring flooding.","Compare plan A and plan B. Which would you fund? what in the chart supports your choice?","Now compare the same two plans from the point of view of the next town downstream. Which plan is better for that town, and does that change the plan you would fund?"],key:["Any workable idea: store water in a reservoir upstream and release it slowly, raise the houses on the lowest street, buy out the lowest street and make it a park, dig a bypass channel around the town, plant the upper watershed so snowmelt is slowed. (4-ESS3-2)","Either plan, judged on the chart. Plan A protects the town most directly but costs the most and pushes the problem downstream. Plan B slows the water and helps the next town too, but gives up fields in wet years. Noticing that plan A moves the effect rather than reducing it is the strongest reasoning. (4-ESS3-2)","B is better for the town downstream, and the chart is the evidence: A sends the same water faster to the next town, while B makes the water arrive downstream later and lower. Then either answer on the funding, rated on the reasoning: a student who keeps A has to say that the county is answering for this town and name what it is handing on, and a student who switches to B has to weigh the fields lost in wet years against the flooding moved downstream. Partial: naming B as better for the next town with nothing said about the funding choice is approaching. (4-ESS3-2)"]}]},{id:24,title:"Power for the Greenhouse",short:"Energy Sources",parts:[{std:["4-ESS3-1"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Study the chart. Answer out loud or in writing.",desc:"describing where energy and fuels come from and how their use affects the environment",html:()=>`<p class="big">The greenhouse and the garden pump can be run four ways.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Option</th><th>Natural resource behind it</th><th>Runs out?</th><th>One effect on the environment</th></tr>
     <tr><td>gas heater in the greenhouse</td><td>natural gas from underground</td><td>yes</td><td>gases into the air</td></tr>
     <tr><td>petrol pump for the irrigation line</td><td>oil pumped from underground</td><td>yes</td><td>exhaust, noise, risk of fuel spills near the beds</td></tr>
     <tr><td>sunlight through the greenhouse glass</td><td>sunlight</td><td>no</td><td>none while it is in use, but the glass had to be made</td></tr>
     <tr><td>a small wind pump over the well</td><td>moving air</td><td>no</td><td>a tall tower, some noise, and nothing on a still day</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Pick two options and trace each one back to the natural resource it starts from.","Sunlight through glass is the cheapest option in the chart. Name the limit on it.","The garden swaps the petrol pump for the wind pump. Name one effect that gets smaller and one new effect that appears."],key:["Any two read correctly: the heater from natural gas underground, the pump from oil underground, the greenhouse from sunlight, the wind pump from moving air. (4-ESS3-1)","It only works while the sun is up and bright, so it gives no heat on a cold night or a dull day, which is when the greenhouse needs it most. A store of heat or a backup is still needed. (4-ESS3-1)","Exhaust, noise and the spill risk beside the beds all get smaller. New effects: a tall tower on the site, some noise from the vanes, and no water pumped on a still day. (4-ESS3-1)"]}]},{id:25,title:"Rolling on the Field",short:"Speed",parts:[{std:["4-PS3-1"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Look at the results. Answer out loud or in writing.",desc:"using evidence to explain how the speed of an object relates to its energy",html:()=>`<p class="big">The same ball was rolled across the field three times into a row of light plastic cones, and the class recorded the speed and how many cones were knocked over.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Roll</th><th>Speed</th><th>Cones knocked over</th><th>Farthest cone moved</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1</td><td>2 meters each second</td><td>1</td><td>14 cm</td></tr>
     <tr><td>2</td><td>4 meters each second</td><td>3</td><td>55 cm</td></tr>
     <tr><td>3</td><td>6 meters each second</td><td>5</td><td>120 cm</td></tr></table>`,qs:["What happens to both measurements as the ball's speed goes up?","Which roll carried the most energy? Name the evidence from the chart.","Explain how this chart shows the link between an object's speed and its energy."],key:["Both go up: more cones are knocked over, 1 then 3 then 5, and the farthest cone moves much further, 14 cm then 55 cm then 120 cm. (4-PS3-1)","Roll 3, at 6 meters each second, because it knocked over 5 cones and moved one of them 120 cm, more than either other roll. (4-PS3-1)","The faster the ball, the more energy it carries, and the more energy it has to pass on to the cones. The evidence is that each faster roll both hit more cones and shifted them farther. Look for the claim with numbers behind it. (4-PS3-1)"]},{std:["4-PS3-3"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Answer out loud or in writing. A prediction needs a reason, not the right answer.",desc:"asking a question and predicting the energy change in a collision",html:()=>`<p class="big">Two balls of the same size are on the grass. A heavy ball is rolling toward a light hollow ball that is sitting still.</p>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("soccerBall",96)}${F.icon("beachBall",96)}</div>`,qs:["Ask one question about this collision that a test could answer.","Predict what happens to each ball when they meet.","The same test is run on a mown lawn and then on long grass. Predict the difference and say why."],key:["Any testable question: how far does the hollow ball travel, does the heavy ball keep going, is the knock louder when the roll is faster, what happens if the hollow ball is rolling instead. (4-PS3-3)","The light hollow ball is pushed forward and moves off quickly. The heavy ball slows down and carries on more slowly. Both halves make the full prediction. (4-PS3-3)","Both balls travel less far in long grass, because the grass rubs against them and takes energy out as heat and as bending the stems. Less energy is left for motion. (4-PS3-3)"]}]},{id:26,title:"Energy in the Greenhouse",short:"Transfer",parts:[{std:["4-PS3-2"],n:4,dur:75,dir:"For each observation, say how the energy traveled. Answer out loud or in writing.",desc:"matching observations to energy transferred by sound, light, heat or electric current",html:()=>`<p class="big">Four things noticed around the greenhouse one spring morning.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th></th><th>What was noticed</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1</td><td>Sun comes through the glass and the dark soil in the beds becomes warm.</td></tr>
     <tr><td>2</td><td>A cord runs from the socket to the propagator mat, and the mat warms the seed trays.</td></tr>
     <tr><td>3</td><td>The watering alarm beeps and the class hears it from outside the greenhouse.</td></tr>
     <tr><td>4</td><td>A metal trowel left standing in a tray of just boiled water becomes hot at the handle.</td></tr></table>`,qs:["How did the energy travel in observation 1?","How did it travel in observation 2?","How did it travel in observation 3?","How did it travel in observation 4?"],key:["By light from the sun through the glass, which the dark soil takes in and becomes heat. Accept light, then heat, and credit a student who names both steps. (4-PS3-2)","By an electric current, through the cord from the socket to the mat, where it becomes heat that then moves into the trays. (4-PS3-2)","By sound, through the air and the glass to everybody's ears. (4-PS3-2)","By heat, from the hot water into the metal and along the trowel to the handle. (4-PS3-2)"]},{std:["4-PS3-4"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Draw your device and label it. Then answer out loud or in writing.",desc:"designing, testing and improving a device that converts energy from one form to another",html:()=>`<p class="big">The seed trays are too cold at night. There is no power at the far end of the garden. Design a device that turns one kind of energy into heat for the trays. You may use a clear lid, dark painted stones, or water jugs. You may also use a mirror, black cloth, or a straw bale.</p>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("sun",96)}${F.icon("sprout",96)}</div>`,qs:["Draw and label your device. Show the energy coming in and the heat reaching the trays.","Name the kind of energy going in and the kind coming out.","You test it and the trays are warm at dusk but cold by dawn. Name one change and say why it should help."],key:["Any labeled drawing with a path: a clear lid over the trays with dark stones or water jugs inside to take in sunlight all day; a mirror aimed to send more sun in. Labels and a path make it a design. (4-PS3-4)","Going in: light from the sun. Coming out: heat in the stones, water and soil. Both forms named, and the change from light to heat is the point of this standard. (4-PS3-4)","Any change with a reason: add more water jugs or stones so more heat is stored for the night, cover the frame with straw or cloth at dusk to slow the heat leaving, paint the store darker so it takes in more sun during the day, close the gaps where cold air gets in. (4-PS3-4)"]}]},{id:27,title:"Ripples, Petals and Signals",short:"Waves",parts:[{std:["4-PS4-1"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Look at the two drawings. Answer out loud or in writing.",desc:"using a wave model to describe amplitude and wavelength and the effect waves have on objects",html:()=>`<p class="big">A petal floats on the pond. The class dropped a pebble in, then a stone twice as heavy, and drew the ripples that passed the petal.</p>
     <div class="figure"><svg viewBox="0 0 520 300" width="520" role="img" aria-label="Two ripple patterns drawn above one another with letters A and B marking parts of the first">
       <text x="10" y="18" font-size="20" font-weight="700" fill="var(--ink)" font-family="Andika,sans-serif">small pebble</text>
       <line x1="20" y1="70" x2="500" y2="70" stroke="var(--line)" stroke-width="2" stroke-dasharray="6 6"/>
       <path d="M20 70 q24 -22 48 0 t48 0 t48 0 t48 0 t48 0 t48 0 t48 0 t48 0 t48 0 t48 0" fill="none" stroke="var(--sea)" stroke-width="5"/>
       <line x1="44" y1="70" x2="44" y2="59" stroke="var(--coral)" stroke-width="4"/>
       <text x="56" y="64" font-size="22" font-weight="700" fill="var(--ink)" font-family="Andika,sans-serif">B</text>
       <line x1="20" y1="112" x2="116" y2="112" stroke="var(--coral)" stroke-width="4"/>
       <text x="68" y="134" font-size="22" font-weight="700" fill="var(--ink)" font-family="Andika,sans-serif" text-anchor="middle">A</text>
       <text x="10" y="186" font-size="20" font-weight="700" fill="var(--ink)" font-family="Andika,sans-serif">heavier stone</text>
       <line x1="20" y1="240" x2="500" y2="240" stroke="var(--line)" stroke-width="2" stroke-dasharray="6 6"/>
       <path d="M20 240 q24 -72 48 0 t48 0 t48 0 t48 0 t48 0 t48 0 t48 0 t48 0 t48 0 t48 0" fill="none" stroke="var(--sea)" stroke-width="5"/>
     </svg></div>`,qs:["Which letter marks the wavelength, and which marks the amplitude?","Which measurement changed when the heavier stone went in? Say whether it grew or shrank.","What does the heavier stone's ripple do to the floating petal, and why?"],key:["A marks the wavelength, from one crest to the next. B marks the amplitude, from the flat line up to the crest. (4-PS4-1)","The amplitude grew. The crests are much taller. The wavelength stayed the same, so the ripples arrive just as often as before. (4-PS4-1)","The petal rises and falls much farther. A bigger amplitude means the wave carries more energy, and a wave with more energy moves a floating object more. The petal bobs in place rather than being carried across the pond. (4-PS4-1)"]},{std:["4-PS4-2"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Answer out loud or in writing. You may draw arrows.",desc:"modelling how light reflects from objects into the eye so the objects can be seen",html:()=>`<p class="big">Yellow and purple flowers are open in the garden bed. At night, with no moon and no lamp, the bed is completely dark and no flower can be seen at all.</p>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("sunflower",96)}${F.icon("purpleFlower",96)}</div>`,qs:["Draw or tell the path the light takes when someone sees a yellow flower in daylight. Name each place it goes.","A gardener shines a flashlight into the dark bed and the flowers appear again. Explain what the flashlight changed.","Does a flower make its own light? Give the evidence from the day and the night."],key:["Light leaves the sun, travels to the flower, reflects off the petals, and travels into the eye. All three legs are needed. (4-PS4-2)","The flashlight supplied light. Now there is light to travel to the petals, reflect off them and reach the eye, which is the whole path that was missing in the dark. (4-PS4-2)","No. In daylight or by flashlight it can be seen, and in complete darkness it cannot be seen at all. A thing that made its own light would still show with every lamp off. (4-PS4-2)"]},{std:["4-PS4-3"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Read the two systems. Answer out loud or in writing.",desc:"comparing two solutions that use patterns to send information",html:()=>`<p class="big">Two groups working at opposite ends of a long field need three messages: tray of seedlings ready, water needed, and come and swap jobs.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>System</th><th>How a message is sent</th><th>Needs</th></tr>
     <tr><td>Kite with a tail</td><td>a kite with one tail, two tails, or flown low and steady</td><td>wind, daylight, and someone looking up</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Handbell</td><td>one ring, two rings, or a long shake</td><td>quiet enough to hear across the field; no wind needed</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Say what the pattern is in each system.","There is no wind at all today. What happens to each system, and which message could still get through?","Design a third system of your own for the same three messages."],key:["In the kite system the pattern is the number of tails and how the kite is flown. In the bell system it is the number of rings and how long the shake lasts. An agreed pattern standing for a message is what carries the information. (4-PS4-3)","The kite cannot fly at all without wind, so none of its messages get through. The bell still works and may even be clearer, because wind is what usually blows the sound away. All three messages could be sent by bell. (4-PS4-3)","Any pattern with three agreed signals: a flag raised to one of three heights, one, two or three bright cloths spread on the grass, a mirror flashed once, twice or three times, three positions of a pole leaned against the fence. (4-PS4-3)"]}]},{id:28,title:"A Planting Tool",short:"Design",parts:[{std:["3-5-ETS1-1"],n:3,dur:60,dir:"Read the situation, then answer out loud or in writing.",desc:"defining a design problem with criteria for success and constraints",html:()=>`<p class="big">Seeds are being planted too deep in some holes and too shallow in others. Two or three go in where one should. The class has one afternoon, dowels, cardboard tubes, tape, string, and nothing to buy.</p>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("sprout",88)}${F.icon("ruler",88)}</div>`,qs:["State the problem in one sentence, in your own words.","Write two criteria that would tell the class the tool works.","Write two constraints from the situation."],key:["Something like: seeds need to go into the bed one at a time at the same correct depth. A problem statement, not a solution. (3-5-ETS1-1)","Any two testable criteria: every hole is within half a centimeter of 2 cm deep, one seed goes in each hole, a row of twenty can be planted in five minutes, a fourth grader can use it standing up. (3-5-ETS1-1)","One afternoon, only dowels, tubes, tape and string, nothing bought. Any two. A criterion written here instead is approaching. (3-5-ETS1-1)"]},{std:["3-5-ETS1-2"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Study the three designs. Answer out loud or in writing.",desc:"generating and comparing solutions against the criteria and constraints",html:()=>`<p class="big">Three designs for the planting tool.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Design</th><th>What it is</th><th>Materials</th><th>Build time</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1</td><td>a dowel with a tape mark at 2 cm, pushed in to the mark</td><td>a dowel and tape</td><td>very short</td></tr>
     <tr><td>2</td><td>a dowel with a cardboard collar fixed at 2 cm that stops it going deeper</td><td>dowel, tube, tape</td><td>short</td></tr>
     <tr><td>3</td><td>a wheeled seeder that opens a furrow, drops one seed and covers it</td><td>a seeder to buy</td><td>order and wait</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Which design breaks a constraint? Name the constraint.","Compare designs 1 and 2 against the criteria. Which do you expect to give the same depth every time, and why?","Add a fourth idea of your own, using only what is on hand."],key:["Design 3. It has to be bought and nothing may be bought, and it would not arrive in the one afternoon. (3-5-ETS1-2)","Design 2, because a collar physically stops the dowel at 2 cm, so the depth does not depend on the planter watching a mark. A tape mark has to be judged by eye every single time, which is where the differences creep in. (3-5-ETS1-2)","Any idea in the materials on hand: a cardboard tube cut to 2 cm used as a depth gauge over the hole, a tube that holds seeds and drops one at a time through a slot, a string knotted at 2 cm tied to a dowel, a board with holes at a set spacing to mark the row. (3-5-ETS1-2)"]},{std:["3-5-ETS1-3"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Answer out loud or in writing.",desc:"planning a fair test with controlled variables and naming a failure point",html:()=>`<p class="big">The class wants to find out whether the marked dowel or the collared dowel gives a steadier depth. They have both tools and a bed of prepared soil.</p>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("ruler",96)}${F.icon("sprout",96)}</div>`,qs:["Plan the test. What will you measure?","Name two things you must keep the same for both tools so the test is fair.","Name one failure point: where your tool is most likely to go wrong first."],key:["Make the same number of holes with each tool, for example twenty, then measure the depth of every hole with a ruler and compare. Looking at how much the depths vary, not just the average, is the strongest plan. (3-5-ETS1-3)","Any two: the same soil, prepared and watered the same way, the same person making the holes, the same number of holes, the same push each time, holes measured the same way with the same ruler. (3-5-ETS1-3)","Any sensible weak spot: the tape mark peels or slides, the cardboard collar softens when damp and slips up the dowel, soil packs into the tube, the dowel splits, hard ground stops the dowel before the collar reaches the surface. (3-5-ETS1-3)"]}]},{id:29,title:"Finding Places on the Map",short:"Regions",parts:[{std:["HSS-4.1"],n:4,dur:90,dir:"Study the chart of four California places. Answer out loud or in writing.",desc:"using map coordinates and landform information to describe California's places and regions",html:()=>`<p class="big">Four places in California, given by latitude and longitude. Latitude counts degrees north, so a larger number is further north. Longitude here counts degrees west, so a larger number is further west.</p>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("globe",80)}${F.icon("compass",80)}${F.icon("map",80)}</div>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Place</th><th>Latitude north</th><th>Longitude west</th><th>Region</th><th>Growing season</th></tr>
     <tr><td>Crescent City</td><td>41.8</td><td>124.2</td><td>Coast, far north</td><td>cool and short, heavy rain</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Fresno</td><td>36.7</td><td>119.8</td><td>Central Valley</td><td>long and hot, irrigation needed</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Bishop</td><td>37.4</td><td>118.4</td><td>east of the Sierra</td><td>short, cold nights, very dry</td></tr>
     <tr><td>San Diego</td><td>32.7</td><td>117.2</td><td>Coast, far south</td><td>long and mild, little rain</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Which place is furthest north and which is furthest south? Use the latitude numbers.","Fresno and Bishop are at almost the same latitude. Explain why their growing seasons are so different.","Which place is furthest west? Say how the longitude numbers tell you.","Name one physical feature and one human feature that would define the Central Valley. Then say what makes each one that kind."],key:["Crescent City is furthest north at 41.8 degrees. San Diego is furthest south at 32.7 degrees. Partial: one of the two places named, or both named with no latitude used, is approaching. (HSS-4.1)","Latitude is not the only thing that matters. Bishop sits high up and east of the Sierra Nevada, so it is cold at night and in the rain shadow, while Fresno sits low on the valley floor where it is hot and can be irrigated. Partial: naming the height with nothing said about the rain shadow or the valley floor is approaching. (HSS-4.1)","Crescent City, at 124.2 degrees west. A larger west longitude number means further west, and 124.2 is the largest in the chart. Partial: the right place named with no account of how the longitude numbers work is approaching. (HSS-4.1)","Physical: flat land, deep soil, two large rivers, hot summers. Human: canals, dams, orchards, highways, farm towns. Physical comes from nature, human is built by people. Partial: one of each named with no difference stated is approaching. (HSS-4.1)"]}]},{id:30,title:"The Year's Work, Then and Later",short:"California Past",parts:[{std:["HSS-4.2"],n:3,dur:200,dir:"Read the chart, then answer out loud or in writing.",desc:"describing the economic and social life and the interactions among the peoples of early California",html:()=>`<p class="big">What each spring and autumn looked like in three periods of early California.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Period</th><th>Spring work</th><th>Autumn work</th></tr>
     <tr><td>First peoples, for thousands of years</td><td>fishing the runs, gathering greens, seeds and bulbs, burning patches of brush to keep meadows open</td><td>the acorn harvest, drying and storing food for winter, trading with other nations, and the ceremonies and dances that followed the harvest</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Spanish mission period, 1769 onward</td><td>plowing and sowing wheat and corn, repairing irrigation ditches, lambing</td><td>harvest, grinding grain, making candles, leather and tiles in the workshops</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Mexican rancho period, 1820s onward</td><td>rounding up and branding the new calves on the open range</td><td>the hide and tallow harvest, drying hides and rendering tallow for the trading ships</td></tr></table>
     <p class="big">Each nation had its own religious beliefs and its own ceremonies, and they were timed by the year's work rather than kept apart from it. Miwok nations danced in a large round earth covered ceremonial house after the acorn harvest. Yokuts nations held a mourning ceremony each year for the people who had died since the last one. The nations did not share one set of beliefs.</p>
     <p class="big">The missions were founded by Franciscan missionaries sent from Spain. Their purpose was to convert Native Californians to Christianity and to teach them Spanish crops and trades, so the missionaries set the mission day and the mission year: the farming seasons on one side and their own calendar of lessons and services on the other. The work itself was done by Native Californians, on land their nations had held.</p>`,qs:["According to the note, who founded the missions and what were they sent to do? Then name the autumn work in each of the three periods.","The first peoples burned patches of brush on purpose. They held their main ceremonies after the acorn harvest. What does the burning tell you about how they managed the land. What does the timing of the ceremonies tell you about how their religious year and their work year fitted together?","The rancho year was built around the trading ships. Explain how that shaped when the work was done."],key:["Franciscan missionaries sent from Spain, whose purpose was to convert Native Californians to Christianity and to teach them Spanish crops and trades. Autumn work: first peoples, the acorn harvest, drying and storing food, trading; missions, harvesting and grinding grain and making goods in the workshops; ranchos, the hide and tallow harvest, drying hides and rendering tallow. Both halves are needed for full credit. Partial: one half without the other is approaching, so the founders and purpose with only one period's autumn work sits there. (HSS-4.2)","They shaped the land deliberately. Burning kept meadows open, which brought more seeds, bulbs and grazing animals, so the harvest the next year was better. This was careful management, not just gathering what happened to grow. On the timing: the religious year and the work year were the same year. Ceremonies came when the harvest was in, the food was stored and people could gather, such as the Miwok dances in the round ceremonial house or the Yokuts yearly mourning ceremony. Both halves are needed for full credit. Partial: one half without the other is approaching, so the burning explained with nothing on the timing of the ceremonies sits there. (HSS-4.2)","Hides and tallow had to be ready when the ships came, so the hide and tallow work was done in the season before the trading visits. The work calendar was set by a market on the other side of the ocean. Partial: naming the ships with nothing said about when the work was done is approaching. (HSS-4.2)"]},{std:["HSS-4.3"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Study the chart, then answer out loud or in writing.",desc:"explaining the economic and political life of California from the Bear Flag Republic through the Gold Rush to statehood",html:()=>`<p class="big">Prices in the gold country, and the government that followed.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Fact</th><th>Detail</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1846</td><td>Settlers at Sonoma declared the Bear Flag Republic, which ended within weeks when the war with Mexico reached California.</td></tr>
     <tr><td>1848</td><td>The war ended, California passed to the United States, and gold was found at Coloma.</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Prices in the camps, 1849</td><td>A single egg could cost as much as a laborer earned in an hour elsewhere. Flour, boots and shovels cost many times what they cost in the eastern states.</td></tr>
     <tr><td>1849, Monterey</td><td>Delegates wrote a state constitution, setting up a legislature, a governor and courts.</td></tr>
     <tr><td>1850</td><td>California was admitted as the 31st state.</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Why were prices in the mining camps so high?","What did the 1849 constitution set up, and why did California need it before statehood?","Put these in order. Explain the chain between them. High prices in the camps. Gold found at Coloma. Farming and shipping grew in California."],key:["Everything had to be carried in over long distances, there were few farms, stores or roads, and thousands of buyers wanted the same scarce goods. Scarce supply and heavy demand pushed prices up. Partial: saying goods were scarce with nothing said about the number of buyers is approaching. (HSS-4.3)","A legislature to make laws, a governor to carry them out, and courts to judge. Congress would not admit a state without a plan of government in place, so the constitution came first. Partial: two of the three parts named, or the three parts with no reason it came first, is approaching. (HSS-4.3)","Gold was found at Coloma, which brought tens of thousands of people and sent prices very high, and those high prices made it worth growing food and shipping goods to California, so farming and shipping grew. The prices are the link between the gold and the growth of other businesses. Partial: the three in the right order with no chain explained is approaching. (HSS-4.3)"]}]},{id:31,title:"Farms, Councils and Ballots",short:"Government",parts:[{std:["HSS-4.4"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Study the chart, then answer out loud or in writing.",desc:"tracing how California became an agricultural power since the 1850s",html:()=>`<p class="big">Why California can grow what it grows, and what had to be built for it.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Advantage</th><th>What it gives</th><th>What had to be added</th></tr>
     <tr><td>A long warm growing season in the valleys</td><td>more than one crop a year in places, and fruit that ripens fully</td><td>nothing, this is the climate</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Deep valley soil</td><td>deep roots and high yields</td><td>plows, levelling, and draining the old marshes</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Mountain snow</td><td>a store of water that melts through spring and summer</td><td>dams, canals and aqueducts to carry and hold it</td></tr>
     <tr><td>A dry summer</td><td>fruit dries well and crops are not spoiled by summer rain</td><td>irrigation, because crops get no summer rain either</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Two advantages in the chart needed almost nothing added, and two needed a great deal. Sort them.","A dry summer is listed as an advantage. Explain how it is both a help and a problem.","Explain how mountain snow and valley farming are connected, step by step."],key:["The long warm season needed nothing added, since it is simply the climate. Deep soil needed plowing, levelling and draining, and mountain snow needed dams, canals and aqueducts. The dry summer needed irrigation, so it belongs with the second pair too. Partial: three of the four sorted right is approaching. (HSS-4.4)","It helps because fruit ripens and dries without being spoiled by rain and crops are not ruined in the field. It is a problem because the crops get no water from the sky in the months they are growing fastest, so all of it has to be brought to them. Partial: one of the help and the problem given is approaching. (HSS-4.4)","Snow falls in the mountains in winter, melts through spring and summer, runs into rivers, is held behind dams, and is carried by canals and aqueducts to valley fields where it waters crops through the dry summer. Four or five steps named in order. Partial: two or three steps named in order is approaching. (HSS-4.4)"]},{std:["HSS-4.5"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Study the meeting notice, then answer out loud or in writing.",desc:"explaining the structures, functions and powers of local, state and federal government",html:()=>`<p class="big">A notice posted outside the town hall.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>City council meeting, item by item</th><th>What the council will do</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1. Repairs to Oak Street</td><td>vote on spending from the city budget</td></tr>
     <tr><td>2. Hours for the public library</td><td>vote on a change</td></tr>
     <tr><td>3. A new crossing on the state highway</td><td>send a request to the state highway department</td></tr>
     <tr><td>4. Public comment</td><td>any resident may speak for three minutes</td></tr>
     <tr><td>5. Report on federal flood grant</td><td>hear how the money from the federal government will be used</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Which items can the council decide by itself, and which cannot? Say why for one of each.","Item 4 lets any resident speak. Why would a council set that aside time on purpose?","Three levels of government appear in this one notice. Name each one and the item it appears in."],key:["Items 1 and 2 it can decide, because city streets and the city library are local responsibilities paid from the city budget. Item 3 it cannot decide, because the highway belongs to the state, so the council can only make a request. Partial: the items sorted right with no reason for either is approaching. (HSS-4.5)","Because the council is chosen by the residents and governs on their behalf, so hearing from them is part of how the decision is meant to be made. It also brings the council information it would not otherwise have. Partial: saying it is polite or fair with no account of the council governing on the residents' behalf is approaching. (HSS-4.5)","Local in items 1, 2 and 4, the city council itself. State in item 3, the state highway department. Federal in item 5, the flood grant from the federal government. Partial: two of the three levels matched to their items is approaching. (HSS-4.5)"]}]}];const WC=FLU.split(/\s+/).filter(Boolean).length;const readPart=STOPS[2].parts[0];readPart.n=WC;readPart.key[0]=readPart.key[0].replace("__WC__",WC);readPart.desc=`a ${WC}-word grade-level passage read aloud`;const LEVELUPS={4:{id:"L4",title:"Level Up: Quote It",std:["RI.5.1"],needs:["RI.4.1"],n:2,dir:"Answer out loud. Quote the text exactly when you explain.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>A gauge is moved from the open field to a spot right beside the gymnasium wall. The readings for the next month are lower than the old spot's. Explain why, and quote the text to back it up.</li><li>Two gauges stand side by side in the same field, but one has a funnel on top that is wider than its tube. Which gauge reads higher, and why? Quote the text.</li></ol>${gaugeHtml(true)}`,key:["The wall shelters the tube and blocks part of the rain, so the reading comes out low. Quotes “A wall or a tree beside it blocks part of the rain and shelters the tube, so the reading comes out low,” and may add “at least twice as far from the nearest tall object as that object is high.” (RI.5.1, quoting accurately to support an inference)","The one with the wide funnel reads higher. It catches rain over a wider opening than the part you read, so the same rainfall fills more depth into the tube. Quotes “A tube that flared out at the top would catch rain over a wider opening than the part you read, and every number would come out too high.” (RI.5.1, quoting accurately)"]},5:{id:"L5",title:"Level Up: Add the Unlike Ones",std:["5.NF.A.2"],needs:["4.NF.A.2"],n:3,dir:"Work on your paper and hold it up. Estimate first, then solve.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>One row is 2/3 planted and another row is 1/4 planted. Both rows are the same length. Altogether, how much of one row's worth has been planted? Estimate with benchmarks first, then work it out.</li><li>A tank was 7/8 full. The class used 1/3 of a tank watering. How much is left?</li><li>Noor says 1/2 + 1/3 = 2/5. Use a benchmark to show without computing that the answer cannot be right.</li></ol>`,key:["11/12. Estimate: 2/3 is a bit more than 1/2 and 1/4 is a bit less, so the total should be near 1 but under it. In twelfths, 2/3 = 8/12 and 1/4 = 3/12, and 8/12 + 3/12 = 11/12. (5.NF.A.2, adding unlike fractions with an estimate)","13/24 of a tank. In twenty-fourths, 7/8 = 21/24 and 1/3 = 8/24, and 21/24 − 8/24 = 13/24. Estimate check: 7/8 is near 1 and 1/3 is near 1/3, so a little over 1/2 is sensible. (5.NF.A.2)","1/2 alone is already 1/2, and 2/5 is less than 1/2, so a sum that starts at 1/2 and adds more cannot land below 1/2. Noor added the tops and the bottoms, which is not how fractions add. The correct answer is 5/6. (5.NF.A.2, using benchmarks to judge reasonableness)"]},6:{id:"L6",title:"Level Up: Quote the Story",std:["RL.5.1"],needs:["RL.4.1"],n:2,dir:"Answer out loud. Quote the story exactly when you explain.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>The story never says that Elena learned to trust the skewer instead of her worry. How do you know she did? Quote two parts of the story.</li><li>Why does Mr. Haddad hold out his wet finger instead of just telling her the answer? Quote the story in your explanation.</li></ol>${F.passage(STORY,STORY_TITLE,true)}`,key:["She had been watering “every day, twice a day, because she wanted to be sure,” and after the rule she “watered the second tray four times in two weeks.” Two quotes that show the change earn the check. (RL.5.1, quoting accurately to support an inference)","He wanted her to see and feel the evidence for herself rather than take his word for it. Quotes “He pushed one finger into the soil up to the knuckle and held it out to her. It came up wet and dark.” Accept the point that he asked “How often?” first, so she supplied the cause herself. (RL.5.1)"]},7:{id:"L7",title:"Level Up: Across the Systems",std:["5.MD.A.1"],needs:["4.MD.A.1"],n:3,dir:"Work on your paper and hold it up.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>A hose fills a 4 liter can in 25 seconds. How many milliliters does it deliver per second?</li><li>The long bench in the back room is 3.5 meters long. A seed tray is 45 centimeters long. How many whole trays fit along that bench, and how many centimeters are left over?</li><li>A bag of soil weighs 5 kilograms. The class uses 250 grams for each pot. How many pots can one bag fill?</li></ol>`,key:["160 milliliters per second. 4 liters is 4,000 milliliters, and 4,000 ÷ 25 = 160. (5.MD.A.1, multi-step problem with converted units)","7 whole trays, with 35 centimeters left over. 3.5 meters is 350 centimeters, and 350 ÷ 45 = 7 with a remainder of 35, because 7 × 45 = 315 and 350 − 315 = 35. (5.MD.A.1)","20 pots. 5 kilograms is 5,000 grams, and 5,000 ÷ 250 = 20. (5.MD.A.1)"]},10:{id:"L10",title:"Level Up: Tense and Tangles",std:["L.5.1"],needs:["L.4.1"],n:3,dir:"Say each answer out loud.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>Fix the tense shift: By the time the bell rang, Elena counts eleven seedlings.</li><li>Which word fits, and what kind of word is it? We watered the trays, ____ the soil was dry. Choose: and, because, or but.</li><li>Fix the sentence: Yesterday Mr. Haddad has checked the vent twice.</li></ol>`,key:["By the time the bell rang, Elena had counted eleven seedlings. The past perfect is needed because the counting finished before the bell. (L.5.1c, verb tense, and L.5.1d, correcting a shift in tense)","because, and it is a subordinating conjunction, since it joins a reason to the main clause. (L.5.1a, conjunctions and their function)","Yesterday Mr. Haddad checked the vent twice. A sentence with a finished time such as yesterday takes the simple past, not the present perfect. (L.5.1d, correcting an inappropriate shift in verb tense)"]}};const PASSAGE_STD=["RL.4.1","RI.4.1","RI.4.9","W.4.8","W.4.2","RL.5.1","RI.5.1"];const MULT_N=12;let multSel=[0,0];function multHtml(){let h=`<div class="tool-scroll"><table class="mult"><tr><th>×</th>`;for(let c=1;c<=MULT_N;c++)h+=`<th data-r="0" data-c="${c}" class="${multSel[1]===c?"hl":""}">${c}</th>`;h+="</tr>";for(let r=1;r<=MULT_N;r++){h+=`<tr><th data-r="${r}" data-c="0" class="${multSel[0]===r?"hl":""}">${r}</th>`;for(let c=1;c<=MULT_N;c++){const hr=multSel[0]===r,hc=multSel[1]===c;h+=`<td data-r="${r}" data-c="${c}" class="${hr&&hc?"pick":hr||hc?"hl":""}">${r*c}</td>`}h+="</tr>"}return h+`</table></div><p class="small">Click a number to light up its row and column. Click it again to clear.</p>`}const FRAC_DENS=[1,2,3,4,5,6,8,10,12];let fracOn={};function fracHtml(){return FRAC_DENS.map(d=>{const on=fracOn[d]||0;let r=`<div class="fs-row">`;for(let k=0;k<d;k++)r+=`<button class="fs-part${d>=8?" sm":""}${k<on?" on":""}" data-d="${d}" data-k="${k}">${d===1?"1":"1/"+d}</button>`;return r+"</div>"}).join("")+`<p class="small">Click a piece to shade up to it. Click the last shaded piece to clear the row.</p>`}function unitHtml(){return F.table(["1 of the bigger unit","equals","of the smaller unit"],[["1 kilometer","=","1,000 meters"],["1 meter","=","100 centimeters"],["1 centimeter","=","10 millimeters"],["1 kilogram","=","1,000 grams"],["1 pound","=","16 ounces"],["1 liter","=","1,000 milliliters"],["1 hour","=","60 minutes"],["1 minute","=","60 seconds"],["1 foot","=","12 inches"],["1 yard","=","3 feet"]])+`<p class="small">Going from a bigger unit to a smaller one, multiply. The count gets bigger because each unit is smaller.</p>`}function areaHtml(){return F.table(["What to find","How"],[["Area of a rectangle","length × width, answer in square units"],["Perimeter of a rectangle","2 × (length + width), answer in plain units"],["A missing side length","area ÷ the side you already know"],["How many pieces fit around","perimeter ÷ the length of one piece"]])+`<p class="small">Area counts the squares inside the shape. Perimeter measures the trip around the outside. Two rectangles can share a perimeter and still have different areas.</p>`}function equivHtml(){return`<ul class="starters"><li>Two fractions are equivalent when they name the same amount of the same whole.</li><li>To build one: multiply the top and the bottom by the same number. 2/5 = (2 × 2)/(5 × 2) = 4/10.</li><li>To simplify one: divide the top and the bottom by the same number. 6/8 = (6 ÷ 2)/(8 ÷ 2) = 3/4.</li><li>To compare two unlike fractions: rewrite both with the same bottom number, then compare the tops.</li><li>Benchmarks: a fraction is more than 1/2 when the top is more than half the bottom.</li></ul>`}const STARTERS={"W.4.1":["The school should ____ because ____.","One reason is ____. For instance, ____.","Another reason is ____.","In order to ____, the school would have to ____.","For these reasons, ____."],"W.4.2":["Water travels through a plant in a path that starts ____.","First, ____.","Then ____, because ____.","A stoma is ____.","By the end of the trip, ____."],"W.4.3":["At first, ____.","Then ____.","“____,” ____ said.","After that, ____.","By the time ____, ____.","In the end, ____."],"W.4.8":["Gets in: ____ (Source 1)","Gets out: ____ (Source 2)","Sources: 1. ____ by ____. 2. ____ by ____."],"SL.4.2":["In my own words, ____.","The notice said that ____.","The reason was ____.","The graph shows ____.","The tallest bar is ____."],"RI.4.9":["Source 1 says ____.","Source 2 says ____.","Putting them together, ____.","That matters because ____."],"SL.4.4":["One time I ____.","To start with, ____.","Then ____.","If you had been there you would have seen ____.","In the end, ____."]};const SENT_ALIAS={opinion:"W.4.1",facts:"W.4.2",story:"W.4.3","W.4.1":"opinion","W.4.2":"facts","W.4.3":"story"};function sentKey(st){const ps=st&&st.parts&&st.parts.length?st.parts:st?[st]:[];const pick=c=>!c?null:STARTERS[c]?c:SENT_ALIAS[c]&&STARTERS[SENT_ALIAS[c]]?SENT_ALIAS[c]:null;for(const p of ps){const a=pick(p.alt&&p.alt.key||p.wk||p.wt);if(a)return a;for(const c of p.std||[]){const k=pick(c);if(k)return k}}return null}function sentHtml(st){const k=sentKey(st);return`<ul class="starters">${(STARTERS[k]||STARTERS["W.4.1"]).map(x=>`<li>${x}</li>`).join("")}</ul>`}function chunkHtml(){return F.table(["Word part","What it means"],[["trans-","across: transplant"],["dis-","apart, away, or not: disappear"],["un-","not: unsteady"],["-ment","the result of an action: measurement"],["-ent","doing or being: absorbent"],["-ling","a small or young one: seedling"],["therm","heat: thermometer"],["meter","a measure: thermometer"],["-ture","says cher at the end of a word: moisture"]])+`<p class="small">Cover the ending with a finger, read the base word, then add the ending back on.</p>`}const TOOLS={chunk:{name:"Word part chart",std:["RF.4.3"],html:chunkHtml},mult:{name:"Multiplication chart",std:["4.OA.A.1","4.NBT.A.1","4.NF.A.1","4.NF.A.2","4.MD.A.1","4.MD.A.3","5.MD.A.1","5.NF.A.2"],html:multHtml,bind:(b,r)=>b.querySelectorAll("[data-r]").forEach(c=>c.onclick=()=>{const x=+c.dataset.r,k=+c.dataset.c;multSel=multSel[0]===x&&multSel[1]===k?[0,0]:[x,k];r()})},frac:{name:"Fraction strips",std:["4.NF.A.1","4.NF.A.2","5.NF.A.2"],html:fracHtml,bind:(b,r)=>b.querySelectorAll(".fs-part").forEach(x=>x.onclick=()=>{const d=+x.dataset.d,k=+x.dataset.k;fracOn[d]=fracOn[d]===k+1?0:k+1;r()})},equiv:{name:"Equivalent fraction card",std:["4.NF.A.1","4.NF.A.2","4.NF.C.6","4.NF.C.7","5.NF.A.2"],html:equivHtml},units:{name:"Measurement unit card",std:["4.MD.A.1","5.MD.A.1"],html:unitHtml},area:{name:"Area and perimeter card",std:["4.MD.A.3"],html:areaHtml},sent:{name:"Sentence frames",std:["W.4.1","W.4.2","W.4.3","W.4.8","SL.4.2","SL.4.4","RI.4.9"],html:sentHtml}};const SUP_CAVEATS=[{t:"chunk",std:["RF.4.3"],msg:"The word part chart names the prefixes, suffixes, and roots and uses words from the list as its examples, so count the word reading as supported, not independent."},{t:"mult",std:["4.NF.A.1","4.NF.A.2","5.NF.A.2"],msg:"The multiplication chart supplies the products used to build common denominators. Rate on whether the student chose the right denominator and explained the comparison."},{t:"mult",std:["4.MD.A.1","4.MD.A.3","5.MD.A.1"],msg:"The multiplication chart gives the products. Rate on the setup, the unit, and the reasoning, not on the arithmetic."},{t:"frac",std:["4.NF.A.1"],msg:"Fraction strips show the equal pieces lined up, so the student can see the equivalence instead of reasoning it out. Rate on whether the student can say what the strips show and write the matching equation."},{t:"frac",std:["4.NF.A.2","5.NF.A.2"],msg:"Lined-up fraction strips let the student see which fraction is longer, so count the comparisons as supported, not independent."},{t:"equiv",std:["4.NF.A.1","4.NF.A.2","5.NF.A.2"],msg:"The card gives the rule for building and simplifying equivalent fractions and the benchmark test, so count those answers as supported, not independent."},{t:"units",std:["4.MD.A.1","5.MD.A.1"],msg:"The unit card gives every equivalence this stop asks for, so the stop then shows only whether the student picks the right operation. Write that on the record sheet."},{t:"area",std:["4.MD.A.3"],msg:"The card gives both formulas, the missing-side method, and the pieces-around method, so count those answers as supported, not independent."},{t:"sent",std:["W.4.1","W.4.2","W.4.3"],msg:"Sentence frames supply the opening, the linking words, and the closing, so count those checks as supported, not independent."},{t:"sent",std:["W.4.8"],msg:"The frames show how to label a note and lay out a source line, so count the source list as supported."},{t:"sent",std:["SL.4.2"],msg:"The frames give the opening of each paraphrase. Rate on whether the content is right and whether the student used their own words."},{t:"sent",std:["SL.4.4"],msg:"The frames supply the opening, the order words, and the closing of the account, so count the organization check as supported and rate the facts, the detail, and the pace."},{t:"mult",std:["4.OA.A.1"],msg:"The multiplication chart hands the student the factor pair, which is the first half of writing the comparison. Rate on whether the student can say it as times as many and write the equation."},{t:"mult",std:["4.NBT.A.1"],msg:"The chart shows the tens column, so the ten-times relationship can be read off it. Rate on the place value explanation, not on the arithmetic."},{t:"equiv",std:["4.NF.C.6","4.NF.C.7"],msg:"The card shows how to rewrite a fraction with a different denominator, which is the move that turns tenths into hundredths. Count the rewriting as supported and rate the decimal notation and the comparison."},{t:"sent",std:["RI.4.9"],msg:"The frames remind the student to draw on each source. Rate on the content of the answer rather than the structure."},{t:"passage",std:PASSAGE_STD,msg:"If the passage was read to the student, this stop shows listening comprehension, not reading."}];return{stops:STOPS,levelups:LEVELUPS,tools:TOOLS,supCaveats:SUP_CAVEATS,passageStd:PASSAGE_STD}}});
